    "knowledge_point": "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration",
    "problem": "Given that ${\\int }_{1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}(f(x)-6x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
    "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(f(x)-6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =1-3({1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =1-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
